      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "The primary objective was to determine the association between beta-2 adrenergic receptor (ADRB2) gene polymorphism (rs1042713, c.46A>G, p.Arg16Gly) and the response to inhaled salbutamol in North Indian children aged 5 to 15 years, with mild to moderate exacerbation of asthma."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "This cross-sectional study was done at a tertiary-care hospital in Northern India from June 2011 to May 2013. 120 children with asthma with mild to moderate exacerbation underwent spirometry at baseline and after administration of three doses of salbutamol. An increase in FEV1 =15% was considered as positive response. Blood samples from these children were analysed for ADRB2 polymorphism (p.Arg16Gly). 94 non-asthmatic adult controls were also studied to determine the prevalence of ADRB2 polymorphism."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "In asthmatic children, the frequency of AA, GG, AG genotypes were 24.2%, 24.2% and 51.7% compared to 20.2%, 20.2% and 59.6%, respectively in the non-asthmatic adults. Salbutamol responsiveness showed no correlation with the studied ADRB2 polymorphism (p= 0.55). A trend towards greater bronchodilator responsiveness amongst AA genotype, compared to GG genotype was observed (Median change in percent predicted FEV1 14.5% and 7.5%, respectively)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "No correlation was found between salbutamol responsiveness and ADRB2 genotype in Northern Indian children with asthma with mild-to moderate exacerbation."
        }
      ]},
